videos.script:
Before the planner reaches a store, both stores ask a question in writing. What data does this app collect, and what does it do with it? {{forms}}

Google calls the answer the Data safety section. Apple calls it the app's privacy details, and shows it on the store page like the label on a food packet. {{labels}} The person reads it before they install.

Two things surprise people the first time. The code you did not write counts. An analytics library or a crash reporter collects in your name, and you declare it. {{sdks}} And the easiest answer to fill in is the one where you collect nothing. {{nothing}}

Today you write the planner's data down as a list: what, why, and where it goes. A small program turns the list into the store's answers, and refuses any entry with no reason. {{inventory}} Then you look at what the planner never needed in the first place.
